WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.596
FedRAMP baselines are often discussed conceptually as Low, tailored low-impact SaaS approaches,

00:00:04.596 --> 00:00:05.720
Moderate, and High.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.841
Lower-impact paths are intended for use cases with limited adverse effects.

00:00:09.841 --> 00:00:14.712
Moderate addresses more serious federal risk and is common for many cloud services.

00:00:14.712 --> 00:00:19.208
High is reserved for use cases where the potential consequences are severe

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:21.831
enough to require stronger expectations for implementation,

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:23.329
evidence, resilience, and operations.

00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:28.575
High differs from Moderate in the level of risk it is meant to address.

00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:30.823
It is not just more paperwork.

00:00:30.823 --> 00:00:34.944
It affects the expected maturity of controls, the rigor of assessment,

00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:39.815
the depth of evidence, the attention to privileged access, the handling of dependencies,

00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:43.561
and the ability to continue or recover operations under stress.

00:00:43.561 --> 00:00:47.683
The baseline has architectural and operational consequences, not just compliance consequences.

00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:51.055
Organizations should avoid choosing High only for market positioning.

00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:55.176
Pursuing a High baseline can shape product design, staffing, automation, documentation,

00:00:55.176 --> 00:00:57.049
customer commitments, and long-term operations.

00:00:57.049 --> 00:01:01.545
If the service does not need High for its federal use case,

00:01:01.545 --> 00:01:03.419
those commitments can become misaligned.

00:01:03.419 --> 00:01:06.041
If the service truly does need High,

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:09.788
the organization must be prepared for the discipline that follows.

00:01:09.788 --> 00:01:13.535
High baseline planning should involve product, engineering, security, compliance, operations,

00:01:13.535 --> 00:01:15.033
customer, and business stakeholders.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.405
The choice affects roadmap decisions, evidence generation, support models,

00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:21.403
recovery expectations, monitoring depth, and shared responsibility language.

00:01:21.403 --> 00:01:25.899
When the baseline is chosen thoughtfully, it becomes a realistic operating target.

00:01:25.899 --> 00:01:30.020
When it is chosen casually, it becomes a source of risk.
